# Lab 5: Docker image vulnerability scanning with Trivy

In this lab I built an image, scanned it with Trivy, made an HTML report, and then fixed the vulnerabilities (bonus) and scanned again.

App source: https://github.com/Ibrahim-Adel15/TrivyImage.git

## Clone the code

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Ibrahim-Adel15/TrivyImage.git
cd TrivyImage
```

## Step 1: Build the image

```bash
docker build -t trivy-lab:v1 .
docker images trivy-lab
```

## Step 2: Install Trivy (RHEL 10)

```bash
sudo rpm -ivh https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy/releases/download/v0.76.0/trivy_0.76.0_Linux-64bit.rpm
trivy --version
```

If I had added the repo file before, I removed it first because it breaks `dnf`:

```bash
sudo rm -f /etc/yum.repos.d/trivy.repo
sudo dnf clean all
```

## Step 3: Scan the image

```bash
trivy image trivy-lab:v1
```

## Step 4: Scan for HIGH and CRITICAL only

```bash
trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL trivy-lab:v1
```

## Step 5: Generate an HTML report

Trivy needs the html template, so I downloaded it first:

```bash
wget https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aquasecurity/trivy/main/contrib/html.tpl
trivy image --format template --template "@html.tpl" -o report-v1.html trivy-lab:v1
```

To open the report I opened it with:

```bash
firefox report-v1.html
```

### What I found in the report (v1)

The image had a total of **222 CRITICAL** and **2133 HIGH** vulnerabilities. They come from two places:

| Source | What it is | Notes |
|--------|-----------|-------|
| OS packages (Debian 11) | The base image is an old, full Debian image | About 2330 findings. Lots of extra packages the app doesn't need (ImageMagick, `libpq-dev`, `linux-libc-dev`, Python 3.9, curl, git...). ImageMagick causes most of the CRITICAL ones. |
| Node packages | App dependencies and the npm tool inside the base image | 1 CRITICAL and 26 HIGH. For example `body-parser` and `path-to-regexp` (old Express), and `tar`, `minimatch`, `brace-expansion`, `glob` (inside npm). |

About half of the HIGH/CRITICAL findings already have a fixed version available.

## Bonus: Fix the vulnerabilities

### 1. Investigate

From the report I saw that the biggest problem is the old and big base image, and then the old Node dependencies.

### 2. Update the base image

In the Dockerfile I changed the `FROM` line to a newer and smaller image:

```dockerfile

FROM node:22-alpine
```

A newer base image removes most of the OS vulnerabilities, and it also comes with a newer npm, which fixes `tar`, `minimatch` and the other npm packages.

### 3. Update the app dependencies

`npm` is not installed on my RHEL machine, so I ran it inside a Node container from the project folder:

```bash
docker run --rm -u $(id -u):$(id -g) -e HOME=/tmp \
  -v "$PWD":/app:Z -w /app node:22-alpine \
  sh -c "npm install express@latest && npm audit fix"
```

This updates `package.json` and `package-lock.json` (Express, `body-parser`, `path-to-regexp`).

### 4. Dockerfile after the fix

```dockerfile
FROM node:22-alpine
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install --omit=dev
COPY app.js ./
EXPOSE 3000
CMD ["npm", "start"]
```

### 5. Rebuild as ivolve-app:v2

```bash
docker build -t ivolve-app:v2 .
```

### 6. Scan again and make a new report

```bash
trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed ivolve-app:v2
trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed --format template --template "@html.tpl" -o report-v2.html ivolve-app:v2
```

## Results

| Image | HIGH | CRITICAL |
|-------|------|----------|
| trivy-lab:v1 | 2133 | 222 |
| ivolve-app:v2 | 14 | 0 |


